1
00:01:27,494 --> 00:01:32,372
اَلْحَمْدُ لله ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلى سَيِّدِنَا محمد وَعَلَى اَلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ وَسَلِّمْ تَسْلِيْمًا كَثِيْراً

2
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:43,487
We'll move on to the next section and that is the Ghazawat(غزوأت), the Fatoohaat(فتوحات ).

3
00:01:45,486 --> 00:01:51,526
The conquests that occurred in the time of Umar Bin Khattab رضي الله عنه .

4
00:01:52,872 --> 00:01:58,120
In our books of history, these would be called a Ghazawat(غزوأت).

5
00:01:59,911 --> 00:02:01,810
What does the word Ghazwa(غزوة) mean.?

6
00:02:02,865 --> 00:02:09,365
Now, the word has a specific meaning in the time of Rasool Allah ﷺ

7
00:02:09,389 --> 00:02:12,346
and then it has a more general meaning after that.

8
00:02:14,112 --> 00:02:20,645
In the time of Rasool Allah ﷺ, battles would be called either Ghazwa(غزوة) or Sariyyah(سرية).

9
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:26,979
Now, originally the word Ghazoo(غزؤ) linguistically means to raid, to attack,

10
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:29,350
that's the meaning of Ghazoo(غزؤ).

11
00:02:32,487 --> 00:02:43,124
However, in the time of Rasool Allah ﷺ this word was used to specifically mean

12
00:02:43,719 --> 00:02:46,980
 a battle in which Rasool Allah ﷺ led.

13
00:02:48,723 --> 00:02:53,954
A battle in Which Rasool Allah ﷺ did not lead is called Sariyyah(سرية).

14
00:02:55,622 --> 00:03:01,127
So, when Rasool Allah ﷺ would send the Sahabah(صحابه) it would be called Sariyyah(سرية).

15
00:03:02,499 --> 00:03:06,337
When he would lead the army himself, it would be called Ghazwa(غزوة).

16
00:03:08,798 --> 00:03:12,038
With one exception and that is Ghazwat Maota(غزوة موته).

17
00:03:13,478 --> 00:03:16,867
Rasool Allah ﷺ was not involved in Maota(موته),

18
00:03:17,296 --> 00:03:28,822
he appointed Zaid Ibn Harisa(زيدبن حارثه) to be the leader and if he is killed then Jafar Ibn Abi Talib(جعفرإبن أبي طالب)

19
00:03:29,806 --> 00:03:32,993
 and if he is killed Abdullah Bin Rawaha(عبدالله بن رواحة).

20
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:39,165
But it was called Ghazwa(غزوة) because of the great importance of that battle.

21
00:03:39,858 --> 00:03:44,215
Since, this was a confrontation between the Muslims and the Romans.

22
00:03:44,239 --> 00:03:46,402
It was a large army of about 3000.

23
00:03:46,775 --> 00:03:50,065
So, it was given the classification of a Ghazwa(غزوة).

24
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:56,011
Later on, after Rasool Allah ﷺ,

25
00:03:56,606 --> 00:04:01,377
in the time of Abu Bakr(ابوبكر), and after that any battle whatsoever would be called a Ghazwa(غزوة).

26
00:04:03,685 --> 00:04:09,053
So, we're now talking about the Ghazawat(غزوات) that occurred in the time of Umar Bin Khattab رضي الله عنه.

27
00:04:10,725 --> 00:04:24,298
In the time of Umar Ibn Al-Khattab(عمرابن الخطاب) the fronts that were opened in the time of Abu Bakr(ابوبكر) carried on.

28
00:04:25,788 --> 00:04:29,675
Abu Bakr Siddique رضي الله عنه opened up two new fronts,

29
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:33,992
the Persian front and the Roman front

30
00:04:34,873 --> 00:04:40,960
and it's quite interesting to see that Abu Bakr Siddique رضي الله عنه who ruled for a little over two years

31
00:04:41,484 --> 00:04:44,230
did all of that within this short span.

32
00:04:46,070 --> 00:04:51,738
He is the one, who led the wars against Al-Murtadden(ألمرتدين), the apostates.

33
00:04:52,523 --> 00:05:02,009
He is the one, who finished it, and then immediately he opened up consciously, by choice,

34
00:05:02,034 --> 00:05:08,477
he is the one, who provoked both the Persian and the Roman Empires simultaneously.

35
00:05:10,469 --> 00:05:14,027
The Roman Empire was in Ash-Sham(ألشام), the Persian Empire was in Al-Iraq.

36
00:05:14,882 --> 00:05:18,898
Abubakar Siddique رضي الله عنه decides that he's going to attack both of them,

37
00:05:19,078 --> 00:05:20,830
and he will do it in the same time.

38
00:05:22,021 --> 00:05:28,166
He started with Al-Iraq which was part of the Persian Empire, and then he moved in to As-Sham(ألشام).

39
00:05:29,838 --> 00:05:41,186
However, these battles were not completed, in the time of Abu Bakr(ابوبكر), death took him away.

40
00:05:42,194 --> 00:05:44,724
So Umar Bin Khattab رضي الله عنه carried on,

41
00:05:47,202 --> 00:05:55,499
Umar Ibn Al-Khattab(عمرابن الخطاب) finished with the opening of Iraq and Ash-Sham(ألشام).

42
00:05:55,765 --> 00:06:02,108
He's the one who opened Jerusalem and then he carried on to further lands.

43
00:06:02,611 --> 00:06:10,461
So, he crossed over to Africa and opened Egypt and parts of Libya.

44
00:06:12,449 --> 00:06:19,225
And also Iran was opened during the era of Umar Bin Khattab رضي الله عنه .

45
00:06:20,637 --> 00:06:23,994
So, we'll start with the Iraqi front.

46
00:06:25,186 --> 00:06:27,916
And this was very concerning to Abu Bakr As-Siddique(أبوبكرالصديق)

47
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:30,525
and in his Wasiah(وصية), before he died,

48
00:06:30,565 --> 00:06:33,613
he gave him Umar Ibn Al-Khattab(عمرابن الخطاب) specific instructions

49
00:06:34,049 --> 00:06:38,565
on carrying on the Jihad Fee-Sabeel-Lillah(فِي سَبيل الله ) in the land of Iraq.

50
00:06:42,626 --> 00:06:49,240
The Persians had a reputation of being tough fighters, they're very strong.

51
00:06:52,510 --> 00:06:59,844
So, Umar Bin Khattab رضي الله عنه was mobilizing the Muslims of Madina

52
00:07:00,012 --> 00:07:06,439
and all over the Muslim world to volunteer for this Ghazoo(غزؤ),

53
00:07:07,317 --> 00:07:13,834
because at that time Ghazoo(غزؤ) was a voluntary

54
00:07:14,217 --> 00:07:19,776
and there wasn't any mandatory recruiting.

55
00:07:23,236 --> 00:07:29,169
Umar Bin Khattab رضي الله عنه is calling upon the Muslims to join the ranks of the army

56
00:07:29,754 --> 00:07:31,346
the first day, nobody shows up

57
00:07:32,116 --> 00:07:36,274
and we're talking about, and we say nobody showed up, we're talking about leaders,

58
00:07:36,299 --> 00:07:39,903
because in those days, people were clans, they were tribes.

59
00:07:40,650 --> 00:07:47,080
So, and these people would usually move together, the bonding was very strong family ties.

60
00:07:47,584 --> 00:07:50,084
So, an entire tribe would come forth,

61
00:07:50,123 --> 00:07:53,338
you know, he didn't really have a lot of individuals coming on their own,

62
00:07:54,248 --> 00:07:59,949
the tribal leader would make a decision, all of his people would follow him.

63
00:08:00,633 --> 00:08:05,199
You'd have obviously some individuals here and there, stepping in

64
00:08:05,454 --> 00:08:09,469
but in general, it was tribes moving along in cohesion.

65
00:08:10,364 --> 00:08:13,936
So, Umar Ibn Al-Khattab(عمرابن الخطاب) is calling for these leaders to come forth

66
00:08:14,134 --> 00:08:15,441
 the first day nobody showed up,

67
00:08:18,166 --> 00:08:24,876
 second day no one shows up, the third day nobody shows up.

68
00:08:27,001 --> 00:08:32,660
Finally Abu Obaid As-Saqafi(أبوعبيدألثَّقفي) who's from At-Taef(اَلطَّائِف)

69
00:08:32,988 --> 00:08:37,375
